# PCLP1 - Homework Assignment 2

Note - this README is formatted in Markdown

## Implementing battleships

My program's battleship logic revolves around the game grids, which store "spaces"
and a pointer to the ship on that space (or `NULL` if there is no ship there).

The space is a `short int`, and its possible values are:

- `n` (with n ranging from 1 to 5) - a ship of length `n`
- `0` (empty space)
- `-1` (hit space / ship)

The `ship_t` pointer is needed to implement the rule that a ship is destroyed
when its head is hit (regardless of how much of the rest of the ship was hit).
Otherwise, it is hard to find the ship head, especially if two or more ships of
the same type are next to each other.

The game grids are not used by themselves, but as part of the `player_data_t`
struct, which stores the data needed for one game. It also stores:

- the player's ships (which are pointed to by the `ship_t` pointers in the grid)
- the number of ships for that game (needed to check the win condition)

---

The main loop goes like so: for each game,

- read game data
- print player's starting grids - using `print_grid()`
- play game (reading player input)

### Reading game data

This is handled by `read_starting_grid()`. It allocates the grids, clears them,
and reads the player's instructions to place the ships.

Ship reading and placement is handled by `read_starting_ships()`. It allocates
each player's ship arrays, and tries to read them from the players, one from
player 1, then one from player 2, and so on, until all the ships have been read.
If a player cannot place a ship at the wanted location, they are prompted for
another ship to place.

Ship reading is handled by `read_ship()`. It has two exit states:

- `SUCCESS`: everything went well, the `ship_t` struct is correctly filled
- `ERR_SHIP_OUT_OF_BOUNDS`: the ship is definitely out of bounds. The `ship_t`
struct is still correctly filled, except for `end_coords`; this is done so that
the calling function can correctly log that the ship placement failed. The read
ship should then be discarded.

### Reading player input and playing game

This is handled by `play_one_game()`, which implements the game loop;
alternating players until one wins:

- read hit coordinates (turning them into 0-indexed coordinates, as the grids 
used internally are 0-indexed, via `get_grid_coords` (in `types.c`))
- hit space (via `hit_space()`), check the result and act appropriately:
    - `HIT_MISS` is logged
    - `HIT_SUCCESS` is logged
    - `HIT_DESTROY` is logged, the ship pointer is removed from the grid and the
number of ships of the hit player is decremented by 1
    - `HIT_REPEAT` is logged
- check if a player won the game (if so, log it and exit the loop)

### Logging

Logging is implemented in `logging.c`.

For an easier time working with strings, I replicated `strdup()`, which is not
available in C99, as `dup_str()` (in `memory.c`).

The logging functions also have to make sure to turn the internally used coords,
which are 0-indexed, into coords indexed from (1, 1). This is done using
`get_output_coords()` (in `types.c`).

## Implementing statistics

Statistics are implemented by extending the code written for battleships.
Helper functions are found in `stats.c`.

Statistics for one game are stored using a `stats_t` struct. To store the stats
for all games, a `stats_t` array is allocated in `main()`.

The main function for handling statistics is `read_and_handle_stats()`. It reads
all the wanted statistics and outputs the computed values for each of them
(via `handle_stat()`). It stops upon reading `Q`.

To be able to accurately display floating point numbers, I wrote
`double_to_str()`, which prints (into a string) the provided number with 15
significant digits (the least required by the C standard to be accurately
represented by a `double`) to have as little rounding as possible when
truncating to 2 digits after the `.`.

Encrypted statistics have not been implemented due to lack of time on my part.

## My thoughts about this assignment

I think that this assignment would've been much more enjoyable if the first task
were easier to implement, but the extra rule (over the typical battleships game)
of "a ship is destroyed when its head is hit" made that more difficult (but did
force us to learn how to implement that efficiently). The same can be said for
the second task, I found it a bit maths-intensive. I cannot extend the same
criticism to the third task, encryption is inherently maths-intensive.

I think that the memory and time constraints imposed for this assignment were
not really in line with the course's philosophy of "memory or time efficiency is
not expected". I understand the technical motives for them, but I think the
actual values for the limits could've been better communicated.

Overall, the assignment was fun, I learnt a lot while solving it and I really
liked the idea of implementing a game.
